p="false" autoFit="no-auto-fit" bold="true" defaultTabSize="47.0047"><p>SYMBOLIC TRUTH</p></content></shape><shape type="text" topLeftX="180.0" topLeftY="424.0" width="466.8" height="10.5"><content fontSize="10.5" fontFamily="思源黑体" color="rgb(148,163,184)" textAlign="left" verticalAlign="middle" wrap="false" autoFit="no-auto-fit" defaultTabSize="47.0047"><p>World Spec (single source of truth) · deterministic simulator · WorldOp version lineage</p></content></shape><shape type="text" topLeftX="743.0" topLeftY="412.2" width="115.7" height="10.0"><content fontSize="10" fontFamily="思源黑体" color="rgb(34,211,238)" textAlign="left" verticalAlign="middle" wrap="false" autoFit="no-auto-fit" italic="true" defaultTabSize="47.0047"><p>single source of truth</p></content></shape></data>
  <note>
    <content textType="body" fontSize="14" fontFamily="Inter">
      <p>Pure neural world models hallucinate and cannot be checked; pure symbolic engines are rigid. We combine them. At the bottom is L1, symbolic truth: a typed World Spec, a deterministic simulator, and versioned WorldOps that can be rolled back. Above it sits the living society of residents. L2 is a small neural layer that imagines consequences and can only advise, never overwrite L1. L3 is the render layer, a playable skin. If the video breaks, the 2D game still runs because L1 keeps the world consistent. This is why our worlds are both generative and checkable.</p>
    </content>
  </note>
